WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A wildlife ranger radios, the missing koala toy is at three,
two.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.640 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Another ranger walks to two, three and finds only a picnic
table.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both heard the same numbers!

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.440 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What information must a map provide so that everyone reaches
the same place?

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.808 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us solve their location mystery.

00:00:17.808 --> 00:00:18.288 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to locate and plot positions using an
ordered pair.

00:00:18.288 --> 00:00:28.155 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:28.155 --> 00:00:32.635 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to locate and plot positions using an
ordered pair.

00:00:32.635 --> 00:00:38.279 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:41.639 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can read the horizontal coordinate first.

00:00:41.639 --> 00:00:44.639 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can plot a position from an instruction.

00:00:44.639 --> 00:00:48.079 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can explain why coordinate order matters.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:51.553 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:54.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You may have used rows and columns to find a seat or a
square on a game board.

00:00:54.113 --> 00:01:01.593 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A coordinate system also describes positions, but here we
locate grid intersections.

00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:07.373 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The axes give two agreed directions, and an ordered pair
tells us how far to move along each one.

00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:09.733 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two axes meet at the origin.

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:12.813 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On our grid its coordinates are zero, zero.

00:01:12.813 --> 00:01:17.653 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The horizontal axis runs to the right, and the vertical axis
runs upwards.

00:01:17.653 --> 00:01:24.463 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We are exploring the first quadrant, so the positions we use
have non-negative horizontal and vertical coordinates.

00:01:24.463 --> 00:01:27.263 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the numbered tick marks before counting.

00:01:27.263 --> 00:01:30.543 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On this grid each interval represents one unit.

00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:30.903 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the spaces between marks, not the number of printed
labels.

00:01:30.903 --> 00:01:42.913 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The same scale continues across the whole horizontal axis
and up the vertical axis, giving a consistent way to locate
positions.

00:01:42.913 --> 00:01:46.193 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To find the position three, two, begin at the origin.

00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:50.273 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Move three grid spaces to the right along the horizontal
direction.

00:01:50.273 --> 00:01:53.433 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This first coordinate tells us the sideways position.

00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:59.185 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We have not yet used the second coordinate, so we should not
stop and claim the position is complete.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:02.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
From that horizontal position, move two spaces upwards.

00:02:02.785 --> 00:02:03.825 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place a point at the intersection you reach.

00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:05.505 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The ordered pair three, two records the complete position.

00:02:05.505 --> 00:02:16.663 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The horizontal-first convention gives both rangers the same
instructions, even when they stand on different sides of a
printed map.

00:02:16.663 --> 00:02:17.223 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We write the pair in brackets with a comma between the
values.

00:02:17.223 --> 00:02:18.783 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first value is the horizontal coordinate and the second
is the vertical coordinate.

00:02:18.783 --> 00:02:29.383 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The pair describes a location, not an addition question.

00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:33.952 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three and two do not mean that every point five units away
will be correct.

00:02:33.952 --> 00:02:36.232 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a marker at six, four.

00:02:36.232 --> 00:02:43.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before the point appears, trace the two movements with a
finger: six spaces horizontally from the origin, then four
vertically.

00:02:43.552 --> 00:02:46.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which intersection should receive the marker?

00:02:46.552 --> 00:02:51.999 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the order clear, and check that you are pointing to an
intersection rather than a square centre.

00:02:56.999 --> 00:03:02.439 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The marker belongs at the intersection six units to the
right and four units up from the origin.

00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:06.839 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read back along horizontal and vertical guide lines to check
it.

00:03:06.839 --> 00:03:13.361 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reliable check uses the axes, rather than remembering only
where a dot happened to appear on screen.

00:03:13.361 --> 00:03:15.041 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now reverse the task.

00:03:15.041 --> 00:03:20.961 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start at the plotted point and trace down to the horizontal
axis to read its sideways position: two.

00:03:20.961 --> 00:03:22.321 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then trace across to the vertical axis to read its height:
five.

00:03:22.321 --> 00:03:30.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The ordered pair is two, five, because horizontal position
is always written first.

00:03:30.018 --> 00:03:34.338 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two, five and five, two use the same numbers, but they name
different points.

00:03:34.338 --> 00:03:37.098 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One is further up; the other is further right.

00:03:37.098 --> 00:03:40.938 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Calling both the same position ignores the agreed order.

00:03:40.938 --> 00:03:46.243 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare them with guide lines and explain which coordinate
tells you the height in each case.

00:03:46.243 --> 00:03:49.603 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point at zero, four sits on the vertical axis.

00:03:49.603 --> 00:03:52.123 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its horizontal movement from the origin is zero, while its
vertical movement is four units.

00:03:52.123 --> 00:03:57.883 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Zero does not mean the position is missing.

00:03:57.883 --> 00:04:02.636 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It gives precise information about a direction in which no
movement is needed.

00:04:02.636 --> 00:04:06.116 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point at four, zero sits on the horizontal axis.

00:04:06.116 --> 00:04:09.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is four units to the right, with no upward movement.

00:04:09.356 --> 00:04:11.236 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare it with zero, four.

00:04:11.236 --> 00:04:18.624 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These examples show clearly why writing coordinates in the
correct order matters, even when one of the values is zero.

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:23.304 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you become confident, you do not need to draw every step
from the origin.

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:23.584 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the horizontal and vertical values to imagine guide
lines that meet at the target.

00:04:23.584 --> 00:04:34.208 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The drawing method may become quicker, but the meanings of
the two coordinates stay exactly the same.

00:04:34.208 --> 00:04:38.288 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A ranger is asked to mark two, five but points to five, two.

00:04:38.288 --> 00:04:41.808 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before we correct the map, explain the mistake in words.

00:04:41.808 --> 00:04:46.528 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which movement did the ranger make first, and which movement
should have come first?

00:04:46.528 --> 00:04:51.382 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a horizontal direction and a vertical direction in your
explanation.

00:04:56.382 --> 00:04:56.582 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The ranger swapped the coordinate roles.

00:04:56.582 --> 00:04:57.622 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To mark two, five, move two units right and five units up
from the origin.

00:04:57.622 --> 00:05:07.302 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The incorrect marker was five units right and two units up.

00:05:07.302 --> 00:05:13.276 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both positions are valid grid points, but only one matches
the particular instruction we were given.

00:05:13.276 --> 00:05:17.516 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw axes from zero to eight using equal one-unit intervals.

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:21.316 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Plot three positions of your choice and write their
coordinates.

00:05:21.316 --> 00:05:24.916 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask a partner to plot the same pairs on a separate grid.

00:05:24.916 --> 00:05:31.524 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If the results differ, compare axis labels, scales and
coordinate order before deciding who should change a point.

00:05:31.524 --> 00:05:37.284 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For each marker, read the coordinates back from the picture
without looking at your original instruction.

00:05:37.284 --> 00:05:40.364 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This provides a second way to check the position.

00:05:40.364 --> 00:05:44.924 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You should be able to explain both values: how far across
and how far up.

00:05:44.924 --> 00:05:48.919 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A dot without this explanation is only part of the evidence.

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:53.039 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal was to locate positions using an ordered
pair.

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:58.239 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The rangers can now agree on an origin, scale and
horizontal-first convention.

00:05:58.239 --> 00:06:02.439 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can read, plot and check a point, including positions on
an axis.

00:06:02.439 --> 00:06:07.730 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Create one final coordinate clue that leads a friend to
exactly one intersection.
